---
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: nut
  namespace: {{ k8s_namespace }}
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: nut
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
  ingress:
{# `| bool` for the reason networkpolicy.yaml.j2 spells out in full: a string
   `-e netpol_baseline_enforced=false` is truthy to bare Jinja and False to Ansible's `when:`. #}
{% if netpol_baseline_enforced | bool %}
    # Four in-cluster callers plus the host-side shutdown chain, all on upsd's :3493.
    #
    # peanut is the web UI in-cluster.
    #
    # home-assistant runs HA's NUT integration. `sensor.ups_power` is live and updating
    # (360.0 at 2026-08-20 01:03). HA sits on daniel-box, nut is pinned to daniel-server, and
    # nut's hostPort binds `hostIP: 127.0.0.1`, so HA's only path is the ClusterIP.
    #
    # uptime-kuma runs the `k3s nut (upsd)` port monitor
    # (uptime-kuma/templates/static-monitors.yaml.j2:103), which dials
    # nut.{{ k8s_namespace }}.svc.cluster.local:3493. It shares daniel-server with nut today.
    #
    # BOTH need a podSelector, and no ipBlock substitutes for one: pod -> ClusterIP -> pod
    # traffic PRESERVES the caller's pod IP, same-node and cross-node alike. It is NOT
    # masqueraded to the cni0 bridge address. Proven from slice 2's own probe log
    # (netpol-baseline-probe-slice2-79xf7 ran on daniel-box and logged `isolated:` for
    # sonarr/radarr/qbittorrent, all daniel-box pods -- same-node calls that a masquerade to
    # 10.42.0.1 would instead have ADMITTED through the baseline's node ipBlocks). So the node
    # ipBlocks below cover host processes only; they never cover these two callers.
    #
    # The node entries are daniel-server's shutdown chain: upsmon runs as a host process there,
    # so it arrives as a node address, not a pod. nut is permanently pinned to daniel-server
    # (nodeSelector in nut/templates/deployment.yaml.j2 -- the UPS is USB-attached there and
    # this never unwinds), so this is not about the pod moving.
    # The two cni0 entries (10.42.0.1/32, 10.42.1.1/32) duplicate netpol_baseline_node_cidrs
    # ONLY ONCE nut carries `netpol-baseline: enforced` and the baseline selects it. During the
    # deploy step that applies this policy the label is not on the pod yet, so in that window
    # they are the only admission a host-side caller has. Do not delete them as "redundant with
    # the baseline" without first confirming the label has landed.
    # The two flannel.1 entries (10.42.0.0/32, 10.42.1.0/32) are NOT in
    # netpol_baseline_node_cidrs and are load-bearing at all times: a cross-node host->pod
    # packet arrives as the SENDING node's flannel.1 address, so daniel-box host processes
    # reaching the pinned pod on daniel-server show up as daniel-box's flannel.1, not its cni0.
    - from:
        - podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: peanut
        - podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: home-assistant
        - podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: uptime-kuma
        - ipBlock:
            cidr: 10.42.0.1/32
        - ipBlock:
            cidr: 10.42.1.1/32
        - ipBlock:
            cidr: 10.42.0.0/32
        - ipBlock:
            cidr: 10.42.1.0/32
      ports:
        - port: 3493
          protocol: TCP
{% else %}
    # OFF. One empty rule admits everything. `ingress: []` would be the exact opposite and deny
    # everything, which is the easiest thing in this file to get backwards.
    - {}
{% endif %}
